$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Optimization process of the intramolecular cyclization reaction mediated
by PIFA using various solvents (CH2Cl2, CH3CN, EtOAc, THF and EtOH) To a
solution of 2-phenyl-3-phenylamino-but-2-enenitrile 1a (4 mmol) in the
solvent (30 mL) was added dropwise a solution of PIFA in the
corresponding solvent (20 mL) within 30 min with stirring at room
temperature. TLC was used to monitor the reaction process until the total
consumption of 1a. The reaction mixture was evaporated to remove the
solvent. To the residue was then added saturated aq. NaHCO3 solution (10
mL), and CH2Cl2 (3 × 20 mL) was used to extract. The combined organic
layer was dried with anhydrous Na2SO4. The solvent was removed under
vacuum and the residue was purified by silica gel chromatography, using a
mixture of PE and EA (10% EA/PE) as eluent, to give the desired products
3a. From the result in Table 1, it can be seen that CH2Cl2 was the most
desirable solvent for this reaction, so all the other such reactions were
carried out in CH2Cl2. 2-Methyl-1-phenyl-1H-indole-3-carbonitrile (3a)
Following the general procedure, 3a was obtained after silica gel
chromatography (10% EA/PE). Yield: 86%, white solid. Rf = 0.50 (EA/PE =
10/90). m.p.105-106 °C; 1H NMR (500 MHz, CDCl3): δ 7.71 (d, J = 8.0 Hz,
1H, Harom), 7.61-7.53 (m, 3H, Harom), 7.34-7.26 (m, 3H, Harom), 7.22-7.19
(m, 1H, Harom), 7.07 (d, J = 8.0 Hz, 1H, Harom), 2.46 (s, 3H, CH3). 13C
NMR (125 MHz, CDCl3): δ 145.9 (C), 137.3 (C), 136.0 (C), 130.0 (CH),
129.2 (CH), 127.8 (CH), 126.9 (C), 123.4 (CH), 118.9 (CH), 116.3 (CH),
111.0 (C), 86.4 (C), 12.6 (CH3). HRMS (ESI) m/z calcd for C16H13N2 [M+H+]
233.1073, found 233.1071.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM yield depends on reaction conditions (solvent and amt. of reagent)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Synthesis of N-substituted indole derivatives via PIFA-mediated
intramolecular cyclization
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Du, Yunfei; Liu, Renhe; Linn, Gregory; Zhao, Kang
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2006), 8(26), 5919-5922

